[acct2-cod4] SDK 0315, 2026-11-07 (coordinator 22:05: "Yes, write 0315 yourself"; posted on the board first).
What it does: a plain UDP socket's recvfrom takes the VDP header off an out-of-band datagram that a VDP socket
sent. On a PC every VDP datagram is plain UDP (0025), so one from a VDP socket can reach a plain UDP socket, which a
360 never lets happen, and that socket's reader takes the 2-byte game data length for the start of the packet.
Call of Duty 4's system link: the host answers the game browser's query (port 1001, plain UDP) from its port 1000
VDP socket, and the browser read the "infoResponse" as a sequenced packet ("127.0.0.1:1000: Got msg sequence
21233663 but connstate (0) is < CA_CONNECTED"; 0x0143FFFF = 21233663), so it listed no game. Only a datagram that
is exactly a 2-byte length equal to what follows, then FF FF FF FF (an out-of-band packet), loses the two bytes;
nothing else changes, and VDP sockets are untouched. The first one per socket is logged. No new setting.
To test: Call of Duty 4 multiplayer, two copies on one PC joining by the game's menus (dev/agents/acct2-cod4/NOTES.md).

